p-module vocabulary.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/codebase-design/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>diagnosing-bugs</name>\n    <description>Diagnosis loop for hard bugs and performance regressions. Use when the user says &quot;diagnose&quot;/&quot;debug this&quot;, or reports something broken/throwing/failing/slow.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/diagnosing-bugs/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>domain-modeling</name>\n    <description>Build and sharpen a project&apos;s domain model. Use when discussing codebase terminology, writing or editing a CONTEXT.md, or recording or editing an ADR.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/domain-modeling/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>done-means-done</name>\n    <description>Complete substantial authorized assignments with a persistent requirements ledger, mandatory remediation of every confirmed defect discovered in the project, executable acceptance evidence, final integration review, and recovery after interruption. Use for implementation, multi-step plans, exhaustive repairs, multiple deliverables, unfinished assignments, or requests to finish everything without repeated continue prompts. Restore the same task when resuming; never substitute a smaller assignment or call partial work complete.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/done-means-done/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>git-guardrails-claude-code</name>\n    <description>Set up Claude Code hooks to block dangerous git commands (push, reset --hard, clean, branch -D, etc.) before they execute. Use when user wants to prevent destructive git operations, add git safety hooks, or block git push/reset in Claude Code.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/git-guardrails-claude-code/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>grilling</name>\n    <description>Grill the user relentlessly about a plan, decision, or idea. Use when the user wants to stress-test their thinking, or uses any &apos;grill&apos; trigger phrases.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/grilling/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>migrate-to-shoehorn</name>\n    <description>Migrate test files from `as` type assertions to @total-typescript/shoehorn. Use when user mentions shoehorn, wants to replace `as` in tests, or needs partial test data.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/migrate-to-shoehorn/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>niblet</name>\n    <description>Keep interface work anchored to the product it belongs to instead of a generic template. Sets a short design contract, builds from the components and tokens already in the codebase, covers the states a surface can actually reach, and closes by looking at the rendered result. Use when building, reworking, or assessing a web or native interface. Trigger with &quot;niblet&quot;, &quot;niblet skill&quot;, &quot;niblet designer ui&quot;, or &quot;niblet review&quot;. Skip backend, CLI, data, and infrastructure work, prose-only tasks, and questions the product&apos;s own design system already settles.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/niblet/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>no-mistakes</name>\n    <description>Validate your code changes through the no-mistakes pipeline - automated code review, tests, lint, docs, push, PR, and CI - before they reach the configured push target. Use when the user asks to run no-mistakes, gate or ship or validate their changes, push safely, asks you to do a task and then validate it, or invokes /no-mistakes.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/no-mistakes/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>ponytail</name>\n    <description>Forces the laziest solution that actually works, simplest, shortest, most minimal. Channels a senior dev who has seen everything: question whether the task needs to exist at all (YAGNI), reach for the standard library before custom code, native platform features before dependencies, one line before fifty. Supports intensity levels: lite, full (default), ultra. Use on ANY coding task: writing, adding, refactoring, fixing, reviewing, or designing code, and choosing libraries or dependencies. Also use whenever the user says &quot;ponytail&quot;, &quot;be lazy&quot;, &quot;lazy mode&quot;, &quot;simplest solution&quot;, &quot;minimal solution&quot;, &quot;yagni&quot;, &quot;do less&quot;, or &quot;shortest path&quot;, or complains about over-engineering, bloat, boilerplate, or unnecessary dependencies. Do NOT use for non-coding requests (general knowledge, prose, translation, summaries, recipes).\n</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/ponytail/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>ponytail-audit</name>\n    <description>Whole-repo audit for over-engineering. Like ponytail-review, but scans the entire codebase instead of a diff: a ranked list of what to delete, simplify, or replace with stdlib/native equivalents. Use when the user says &quot;audit this codebase&quot;, &quot;audit for over-engineering&quot;, &quot;what can I delete from this repo&quot;, &quot;find bloat&quot;, &quot;ponytail-audit&quot;, or &quot;/ponytail-audit&quot;. One-shot report, does not apply fixes.\n</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/ponytail-audit/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>ponytail-debt</name>\n    <description>Harvest every `ponytail:` comment in the codebase into a debt ledger, so the deliberate shortcuts and deferrals ponytail leaves behind get tracked instead of rotting into &quot;later means never&quot;. Use when the user says &quot;ponytail debt&quot;, &quot;/ponytail-debt&quot;, &quot;what did ponytail defer&quot;, &quot;list the shortcuts&quot;, &quot;ponytail ledger&quot;, or &quot;what did we mark to do later&quot;. One-shot report, changes nothing.\n</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/ponytail-debt/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>ponytail-gain</name>\n    <description>Show ponytail&apos;s measured impact as a compact scoreboard: less code, less cost, more speed, from the benchmark medians. One-shot display, not a persistent mode, and not a per-repo number. Trigger: /ponytail-gain, &quot;ponytail gain&quot;, &quot;what does ponytail save&quot;, &quot;show ponytail impact&quot;, &quot;ponytail scoreboard&quot;.\n</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/ponytail-gain/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>ponytail-help</name>\n    <description>Quick-reference card for all ponytail modes, skills, and commands. One-shot display, not a persistent mode. Trigger: /ponytail-help, &quot;ponytail help&quot;, &quot;what ponytail commands&quot;, &quot;how do I use ponytail&quot;.\n</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/ponytail-help/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>ponytail-review</name>\n    <description>Code review focused exclusively on over-engineering. Finds what to delete: reinvented standard library, unneeded dependencies, speculative abstractions, dead flexibility. One line per finding: location, what to cut, what replaces it. Use when the user says &quot;review for over-engineering&quot;, &quot;what can we delete&quot;, &quot;is this over-engineered&quot;, &quot;simplify review&quot;, or invokes /ponytail-review. Complements correctness-focused review, this one only hunts complexity.\n</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/ponytail-review/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>prototype</name>\n    <description>Build a throwaway prototype to answer a design question. Use when the user wants to sanity-check whether a state model or logic feels right, or explore what a UI should look like.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/prototype/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>research</name>\n    <description>Investigate a question against high-trust primary sources and capture the findings as a Markdown file in the repo. Use when the user wants a topic researched, docs or API facts gathered, or reading legwork delegated to a background agent.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/research/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>resolving-merge-conflicts</name>\n    <description>Use when you need to resolve an in-progress git merge/rebase conflict.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/resolving-merge-conflicts/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>scaffold-exercises</name>\n    <description>Create exercise directory structures with sections, problems, solutions, and explainers that pass linting. Use when user wants to scaffold exercises, create exercise stubs, or set up a new course section.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/scaffold-exercises/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>setup-pre-commit</name>\n    <description>Set up Husky pre-commit hooks with lint-staged (Prettier), type checking, and tests in the current repo. Use when user wants to add pre-commit hooks, set up Husky, configure lint-staged, or add commit-time formatting/typechecking/testing.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/setup-pre-commit/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>source-command-de-sloppify</name>\n    <description>Migrated source command `de-sloppify`</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/source-command-de-sloppify/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>source-command-evolve</name>\n    <description>Migrated source command `evolve`</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/source-command-evolve/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>source-command-learn</name>\n    <description>Migrated source command `learn`</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/source-command-learn/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>source-command-learn-eval</name>\n    <description>Extract reusable patterns from the session, self-evaluate quality before saving, and determine the right save location (Global vs Project).</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/source-command-learn-eval/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>tdd</name>\n    <description>Test-driven development. Use when the user wants to build features or fix bugs test-first, mentions &quot;red-green-refactor&quot;, or wants integration tests.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/tdd/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>to-bug</name>\n    <description>Autonomous bug route of /to-goal: same control plane, worktree isolation, ledger, budgets, and review, with the route pinned to diagnosing-bugs and the bug fast path armed, so a reproducible bug goes red → regression test → fix → review → hand back without research, grilling, spec, or tickets unless the diagnosis proves it needs them. Use on /to-bug &lt;symptom | failing command | issue ref&gt; or $to-bug.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/to-bug/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>to-goal</name>\n    <description>Autonomous software-factory pipeline over the mattpocock/skills flow map plus research-stack, defensive-design, and zero-tech-debt: route, on-ramp, conditional research, self-answered grilling, to-spec, to-tickets, implement-spec task-graph build in parallel worktrees, code-review, retro, with no pauses. Use on /to-goal &lt;objective&gt; or $to-goal, when the user wants a feature or skill delivered fully autonomously, or under a loop.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/to-goal/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>to-new</name>\n    <description>Greenfield route of /to-goal: create a new project, package, service, or agent skill from nothing, autonomously. Creates the directory and repository when none exists, scaffolds a runnable baseline so the environment contract holds, researches stack and versions up front through research-stack, maps the effort with wayfinder, then runs the same grill → spec → tickets → build → review → retro pipeline on the same control plane. Use on /to-new &lt;thing to create&gt; or $to-new.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/to-new/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>to-orc</name>\n    <description>Delegation-only orchestrator: plan, dispatch, gate, and judge a task through four sequential phases (scouting → researching → implementing → verification), executing nothing yourself. Every worker is pi running zai/glm-5.3-flash at thinking max, dispatched through the pi-delegate relay, and every dispatch returns a machine-readable status the orchestrator judges instead of trusting prose. Ends with one raw JSON assessment (PASS / REVISE / FAIL). Use on /to-orc &lt;task&gt; or $to-orc, or when the user wants work delegated under strict phase control with an evidence-backed verdict.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/to-orc/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>wizard</name>\n    <description>Generate an interactive bash wizard that walks a human through steps only they can perform. Use when provisioning infrastructure, setting up credentials or CI secrets, walking an unfamiliar third-party dashboard, or running a one-off migration or cutover. Don&apos;t invoke this for steps the agent can perform itself.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/wizard/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>writing-for-agents</name>\n    <description>Writing documents for agents. Use when creating or editing skills, or modifying AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.agents/skills/writing-for-agents/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>council-mode</name>\n    <description>Run a bounded supervisor-mediated advisor council. Use when the user asks for council mode, asks to convene advisors, debate a decision, cross-examine recommendations, or run /council.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/pi-subagents/skills/council-mode/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>pi-subagents</name>\n    <description>Delegate to builtin or custom subagents for single-agent handoffs, parallel\nreview, scripted chaining, async work, forked context, and coordinated\nworkflows. Use when one parent agent should stay in control while children\nsupply focused context, planning, review, or execution.\n</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/.pi/agent/npm/node_modules/pi-subagents/skills/pi-subagents/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>mcp-scripting</name>\n    <description>Write mcpScript JavaScript for discovering, inspecting, and calling MCP tools.</description>\n    <location>/Users/panda/Projects/open-source/tools/pi-mcp-adapter/skills/mcp-scripting/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n</available_skills>\nCurrent working directory: /Users/panda/Projects/test-local/grok-bot"},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"hi"}]},{"role":"assistant","content":"Hi! I'm ready to help — we're in `~/Projects/test-local/grok-bot`. What would you like to do?","reasoning_content":"Just a greeting. Keep it short."},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"start rebuild source code for @Grok_Bot_0.44.0.dmg  use refrence from @refrence/ You are an expert reverse-engineer and macOS systems software architect. Your goal is to guide the extraction, inspection, and high-fidelity source code reconstruction of an application contained within a target `.dmg` disk image.\n\nFollow this systematic workflow:\n\n#### Phase 1: Artifact Extraction & Triage\n1. Mount the `.dmg` via command line (`hdiutil attach path/to/file.dmg`) and inspect the filesystem layout.\n2. Locate the main `.app` bundle under `/Volumes/<MountPoint>/`.\n3. Inventory key directories:\n   - `Contents/Info.plist`: Identify bundle identifiers, executable names, and minimum OS targets.\n   - `Contents/MacOS/`: Identify all binary executables and Mach-O files.\n   - `Contents/Frameworks/` & `Contents/PlugIns/`: Enumerate internal and third-party dependencies.\n   - `Contents/Resources/`: Inspect bundled assets, UI files (`.nib`, `.storyboardc`), and runtime scripts.\n\n#### Phase 2: Tech Stack Identification\nDetermine the core application runtime:\n- **Web/Cross-Platform:** Check for Electron (`Resources/app.asar` or `app/`), React Native, Flutter, or Tauri runtime bundles.\n- **Interpreted/Bytecode:** Check for Python bundles (PyInstaller, py2app) or JVM artifacts (`.jar`).\n- **Native Mach-O:** Run `file` and `lipo -info` on the main executable to check architectures (x86_64, arm64).\n\n#### Phase 3: Extraction & Decompilation Strategy\nBased on the runtime identified in Phase 2:\n- **If Electron/JS:**\n  - Extract `.asar` archives using `npx @electron/asar extract`.\n  - Format, de-obfuscate, and restore module layout for all extracted JavaScript/TypeScript files.\n- **If Native (Swift / Objective-C):**\n  - Run `class-dump` or `dsdump` to extract Objective-C / Swift runtime class interfaces, ivars, protocols, and method signatures into corresponding header files (`.h`).\n  - Extract string references, imported symbols, and export tables using `otool`, `nm`, or `strings`.\n  - Use decompilation analysis (e.g., via Ghidra, Hopper, or IDA Pro) to reconstruct control flow, core logic, and model structures into idiomatic Swift or modern C/Objective-C equivalents.\n- **If UI Assets:**\n  - Convert asset catalogs (`Assets.car`) using `assetutil` or extraction tools to retrieve raw vector and raster assets.\n\n#### Phase 4: Project Scaffold & Reconstruction\n1. Construct a clean build configuration (`Package.swift`, `CMakeLists.txt`, `package.json`, or an `XcodeProj` structure matching the detected framework).\n2. Generate placeholder implementations and structural interfaces matching the discovered class hierarchies and API endpoints.\n3. Iteratively fill in the business logic, state management, and network layers based on the disassembled control flow.\n4. Document all missing dependencies, dynamic linking requirements, and binary-only third-party frameworks.\n\nBegin by requesting the target file path and terminal output for `hdiutil attach` and `file Contents/MacOS/<Executable>`."}]},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"reasoning_content":"The user has attached files? They mention @Grok_Bot_0.44.0.dmg and @refrence/ — likely files in the working directory. Let me check the working directory to find them. This is a reverse engineering task — there's an ida-reverse skill and macos-reverse skill. The prompt says use skills when task matches. macos-reverse skill is relevant. But first let me look at files.\n\nLet me start by listing the directory.","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_3e4d4f1b4dcf0c8ce9e85f85dee439d0","type":"function","function":{"name":"bash","arguments":"{\"command\":\"ls -la \\\"/Users/panda/Projects/test-local/grok-bot/\\\" 2>/dev/null; echo ---; ls -la \\\"/Users/panda/Projects/test-local/grok-bot/refrence/\\\" 2>/dev/null\"}"}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"total 277280\ndrwxr-xr-x  7 panda  staff        224 Sep 14 14:46 .\ndrwxr-xr-x  8 panda  staff        256 Sep 14 00:43 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x  5 panda  staff        160 Sep 14 14:42 .agents\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 panda  staff      12292 Sep 14 14:46 .DS_Store\ndrwxr-xr-x@ 8 panda  staff        256 Sep 14 01:07 analysis\n-rw-r--r--@ 1 panda  staff  141947035 Sep  8 19:13 Grok_Bot_0.44.0.dmg\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 panda  staff         96 Sep 14 14:46 refrence\n---\ntotal 0\ndrwxr-xr-x   3 panda  staff   96 Sep 14 14:46 .\ndrwxr-xr-x   7 panda  staff  224 Sep 14 14:46 ..\ndrwxrwxr-x@ 22 panda  staff  704 Aug 23 16:52 grok-bot-0.18-reconstructed-main\n","tool_call_id":"call_3e4d4f1b4dcf0c8ce9e85f85dee439d0"},{"role":"assistant","content":"There's prior work here — an `analysis/` dir and a reconstructed 0.18 reference project. Let me survey both, then load the macos-reverse skill.","reasoning_content":"There's an existing `analysis` directory and a reference project `refrence/grok-bot-0.18-reconstructed-main`. Let me inspect both. Also the macos-reverse skill should be loaded. Let me first survey.","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_b7700e77cb95a43abae589816384cc4d","type":"function","function":{"name":"bash","arguments":"{\"command\":\"cd \\\"/Users/panda/Projects/test-local/grok-bot\\\"; ls -R analysis | head -60; echo ---; ls refrence/grok-bot-0.18-reconstructed-main | head -40; echo ---; find refrence -maxdepth 2 -name \\\"*.md\\\" | head\"}"}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"app.asar\napp.asar.unpacked\nextracted\nRE-REPORT.md\nsections\n\nanalysis/app.asar.unpacked:\ndist\n\nanalysis/app.asar.unpacked/dist:\ndeps\nnative\n\nanalysis/app.asar.unpacked/dist/deps:\ncursor-proclist\nnode-addon-api\nnode-gyp-build\nruntime-deps-manifest.json\ntree-sitter\ntree-sitter-bash\nweb-tree-sitter\n\nanalysis/app.asar.unpacked/dist/deps/cursor-proclist:\nbuild\nindex.js\npackage.json\nrebuild-with-lock.js\n\nanalysis/app.asar.unpacked/dist/deps/cursor-proclist/build:\nRelease\n\nanalysis/app.asar.unpacked/dist/deps/cursor-proclist/build/Release:\ncursor_proclist.node\n\nanalysis/app.asar.unpacked/dist/deps/node-addon-api:\nindex.js\npackage-support.json\npackage.json\n\nanalysis/app.asar.unpacked/dist/deps/node-gyp-build:\nbin.js\nbuild-test.js\nindex.js\nnode-gyp-build.js\noptional.js\npackage.json\n\nanalysis/app.asar.unpacked/dist/deps/tree-sitter:\nbuild\nindex.js\npackage.json\n\nanalysis/app.asar.unpacked/dist/deps/tree-sitter/build:\nRelease\n\nanalysis/app.asar.unpacked/dist/deps/tree-sitter/build/Release:\ntree_sitter_runtime_binding.node\n\nanalysis/app.asar.unpacked/dist/deps/tree-sitter-bash:\nbindings\n---\nCONTRIBUTING.md\ndocs\nfrontend\nmanifests\nNOTICE.md\npackage-lock.json\npackage.json\npatches\nPROVENANCE.md\nREADME.md\nresearch-archives\nscripts\nSECURITY.md\nsource\nsrc\ntests\n---\nrefrence/grok-bot-0.18-reconstructed-main/PROVENANCE.md\nrefrence/grok-bot-0.18-reconstructed-main/README.md\nrefrence/grok-bot-0.18-reconstructed-main/CONTRIBUTING.md\nrefrence/grok-bot-0.18-reconstructed-main/NOTICE.md\nrefrence/grok-bot-0.18-reconstructed-main/SECURITY.md\n","tool_call_id":"call_b7700e77cb95a43abae589816384cc4d"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_7fce00d8d0d5a9d4ff52e43055d956a9","type":"function","function":{"name":"bash","arguments":"{\"command\":\"cd \\\"/Users/panda/Projects/test-local/grok-bot\\\"; cat analysis/RE-REPORT.md | head -100; echo ===; ls analysis/extracted analysis/sections 2>/dev/null | head -30\"}"}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"# Grok Bot 0.44.0 — Clean Reverse-Engineering Report\n\n**Target:** `grok-bot/Grok_Bot_0.44.0.dmg` (141.9 MB, zlib DMG, APFS)\n**Method:** offline static analysis (no execution of the app). Skills: `reverse-skill-main/skills/macos-reverse` (PRIMARY, case `work/grok-bot-re`, offline-sample granted) + JS/asar + Mach-O tooling.\n**Evidence convention:** every claim in the section files is tagged `[observed]` (string/byte seen in a named file) or `[inferred]` (interpretation + reasoning). Nothing is hand-waved; unknowns are listed per section.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Verdict (lead with impact)\n\n`Grok Bot` is **Anysphere's \"Cursor\" desktop-agent codebase, re-branded (\"sand\" codename) and pointed at x.ai's Grok models** — a cloud+local autonomous bot platform: bots that run on cloud boxes (\"Grok Bot's Computer\") or the local machine, driven by tools (shell/file/MCP/computer-use), with Slack/Teams/iMessage/SCM channels, bot templates shared via `grokbot://` deep links, voice calls over `wss://api.x.ai/`, and a Rust FIDO2 signing daemon for passkey delegation. The Cursor lineage leaks everywhere: bundle id `com.anysphere.sand`, `@anysphere/*` workspace deps, `cursor.com` homepage, `cursor-access-token`/`cursor-refresh-token` secret keys, `cursor://anysphere.cursor-mcp/oauth/return` MCP OAuth redirect, `review.cursor.com`, and the Cursor `checksum` request-signing algorithm — all inside a package whose author is \"SpaceXAI\".\n\n**Why this matters:** the app is a full agent-harness product (not a thin Grok chat client): 5,594 protobuf messages, 6 RPC services with 1,263 methods, a 40-tool local exec daemon, a 98-method coordinator, 111 structured `SAND-E*` error codes, and a 2,248-string i18n catalog. Everything a clean-room reimplementation needs is in the five sections below.\n\n## 2. Package & provenance\n\n| Layer | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Signed by | Developer ID Application: **Anysphere Incorporated (DCNK4UB866)**, notarized, Hardened Runtime (flags `0x10000`), entitlements: camera, audio-input, apple-events, JIT only |\n| Bundle | `com.anysphere.sand`, v0.44.0, LSMinimumSystemVersion 12.0, schemes `grokbot` + `sand` |\n| Runtime | Electron **42.1 / Chrome 148** (universal; main binary arm64 thin) |\n| Payload | `Resources/app.asar` (JS, ~41 MB dist) + `app.asar.unpacked` (native binaries, ~7 MB) + Electron Framework + Squirrel + Mantle/ReactiveObjC |\n| Entry | `main.cjs` (V8 code-cache bootstrap, buildId `7e1506f2-…`) → `main-core.cjs` (1.7 MB) → `main-app.cjs` (2.0 MB) → forked `utilityProcess` **coordinator** + spawned **local-exec-daemon** |\n| Tracks | `stable → \"sand\"`, `nightly → \"sand-nightly\"`, `dogfood → \"sand-dogfood\"` (update feed `https://api2.cursor.sh/updates`) |\n\n## 3. Architecture (process graph)\n\n```\nGro k Bot.app (Electron main)\n ├── main-core.cjs  — secret store (safeStorage → sand-secrets.json), deep-link router,\n │                    update gate (SAND-E05xx), boot snapshot IPC, sandbox scheme registry\n ├── main-app.cjs   — windows, ConnectRPC backend client (checksum/ghost-mode headers),\n │                    voice-call credential minting, template share URLs, tracing\n ├── node-agent-coordinator/main.cjs  (utilityProcess, 3 MessageChannels: control/data/main-data)\n │      · 98 methods; capability broker routing secrets/webauthn/cookies into main\n │      · SSE fan-out: transcript, agents, tray, subagents, async-tasks, computer-action, …\n │      · WebAuthn proxy: /webauthn/requests (SSE) + /webauthn/responses (POST)\n └── local-exec-daemon/main.cjs (detached child, Node IPC bootstrap {fileKeyBase64, computerId})\n        · 40-tool exec surface (shell/read/grep/MCP/computerUse/subagent/pi*/allowlist prechecks)\n        · sandbox: insecure_none | workspace_readonly | workspace_readwrite via helper binary\n          `--policy <json> -- <cmd>` (darwin seatbelt; linux landlock; win32 proxy-only)\n        · self-GC via decideLocalExecDaemonOwnership (supervisor window 90 s, lease, inflight grace)\n        · 5 lease/discovery JSON files under ~/.sand / ~/.cursor/<flavor>\n\nsand-webauthn-signer (Rust, IOKit HID) — CTAP2/FIDO2 assertion signer, stdin JSON + stderr [signer-event]\nsand-op-launcher      — 1Password CLI gate: code-signature check (OU 2BUA8C4S2C) + Seatbelt deny of 1Password group container\nCUGrokBotService      — computer-use sidecar app (JSON-RPC over unix socket, ffmpeg screen recording)\npreload.cjs           — exposes exactly 2 globals: desktop (189 RPC methods) + coordinatorPort\npreload-vnc.cjs       — noVNC liveness beacon + clipboard mirror (cloud-box screen preview)\nrenderer (React 19 + TipTap + mermaid/kaTeX/cynefin) — 2,248-string UI, 32 settings surfaces\n```\n\n## 4. Backend surface\n\n- **Connect RPC** `/aiserver.v1.<Service>/<Method>`, base `CURSOR_API_BASE_URL` || `https://api2.cursor.sh`; Statsig ingest `https://api3.cursor.sh/tev1/v1`.\n- **Services (6, 1,263 methods):** DashboardService 660 · BackgroundComposerService 226 · AiService 194 · **GrokBotService 136** · SandBoxService 39 (strict subset of GrokBotService) · AnalyticsService 8. Proto scale: `aiserver.v1` 4,447 msgs, `agent.v1` 950, `anyrun.v1` 171 (= in-box process supervisor: CreateProcess, restart-backoff, pod metrics — matches daemon's `x-anyrun-network-token` header), `internapi.v1` 3.\n- **Two execution homes:** `GrokBotAgentHarnessKind{BOX, TEMPORAL}`; `GrokBotTemporalHarnessMode{OFF, SHADOW, LIVE, BOX}`; failure hints are server headers (`x-automation-failure-hint`, the ~40 `CLOUD_AGENT_*` codes) — the client enumerates, the backend mints.\n- **Models:** `grok-4.5` (maxMode, effort=high), `grok-4.6`, voice `grok-voice-latest` / `grok-voice-think-fast-2.0` (minted via backend, dial-locked to `wss://api.x.ai/`), `grok-bot-computer-use` (+lab/dev variants), `grok-transcribe` (24 kHz `audio/pcm`). Plans: Grok Bot Plan / SuperGrok Lite/Plus/Heavy; on-demand spend via Cursor dashboard.\n- **Limits:** `grok_bot_conversation_size_limits{256 MB soft / 1 GB hard}`, per-tool concurrency via `SAND_LOCAL_EXEC_ACCOUNT_CHANNEL_LIMIT`.\n- **Secrets shipped in bundle:** OAuth client IDs `KbZUR41cY7W6zRSdpSUJ7I7mLYBKOCmB` (prod) / `OzaBXLClY5CAGxNzUhQ2vlknpi07tGuE` (staging); Statsig client key `client-Bm4HJ0aDjXHQVsoACMREyLNxm5p6zzuzhO50MgtoT5D`; Sentry DSN `https://9fb7a1b8cb70c207a28a00476311bd40@metrics.cursor.sh/4511747394240513`. Gateway contract via `SAND_HOST_GATEWAY_{URL,TOKEN,NETWORK_TOKEN}`, persisted `gateway(-descriptor).json`; \"forever-box\" hand-back flow.\n- **Auth:** secret-store keys are literal Cursor leftovers (`cursor-access-token`, `cursor-refresh-token`, `cursor-selected-team-id`, `cursor-accounts`, `cursor-account-profile` in `sand-secrets.json`); envelope `scoped:v1:<sha256(jwt.sub)>:<base64>`, `plaintext:v1:` fallback, in-memory-only when no keychain.\n\n## 5. Security-relevant findings\n\n1. **Renderer containment is tight:** main window `contextIsolation:true, nodeIntegration:false, sandbox:true`; navigation gated (`deny` + external opener); CSP `connect-src 'self' ws: sand-media:` — note **`ws:` allows WebSocket to any host**, and `img-src … https:` allows arbitrary remote images (both flagged in sec-render).\n2. **Chrome cookie import is a full re-implementation of Chrome's cookie decryption** (keychain `Chrome Safe Storage` → PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA1 \"saltysalt\" ×1003 → AES-128-CBC, `v10/v11` blobs), with a **TCC workaround**: on EPERM it copies files via Finder (`osascript duplicate`) and classifies failures `finder-automation | chrome-safe-storage | full-disk-access`. Mitigation exists: cookie-origin approval stream defaulting **deny**. Entitlements are minimal — no keychain-access-group — so this relies on user Full Disk Access prompts.\n3. **WebAuthn proxy**: remote box passkey ceremonies are relayed to the local machine; the Rust signer **refuses credential creation** (`create_unsupported` — assertions only), ceremonies require an explicit user grant prompt, 60 s channel timeout, abort = SIGTERM. Default `webauthnProxyEnabled:true`.\n4. **Sandbox helper binary is runtime-resolved and absent from the DMG** (not in `app.asar.unpacked/dist/native`) — policies (`workspace_readwrite` etc.) are enforced by a helper fetched/installed elsewhere; Windows gets proxy-only, no filesystem isolation. Preflight = `-- /bin/true`.\n5. **Sudo escalation** via askpass helper: injected `alias sudo='sudo -A'` + `SUDO_ASKPASS`/`CURSOR_ASKPASS_SOCKET`/`CURSOR_ASKPASS_SECRET` (unix socket + shared secret).\n6. **Preload stubs JS dialogs**: `confirm` → always true, `prompt` → null, `alert` → noop — any agent-driven page sees consent dialogs as approved. Flagged as a footgun.\n7. **Rebrand gaps** (operational, not exploitable): MCP OAuth redirect `cursor://anysphere.cursor-mcp/oauth/return` + loopback `:8787/callback` under a bundle that only registers `grokbot`/`sand`.\n\n## 6. Feature surface (user-visible)\n\nBots with configurable models/permissions; cloud box + local computer; canvas/kanban/mermaid/cynefin rendering; TipTap composer; attachments (PDF/xlsx/docx/mammoth); **bot templates** (`grokbot://app/v1/bot-template` deep links, `https://x.ai/bot/<id>` share, visibility public/team); **channels** Slack/Teams/iMessage; **SCM listeners** GitHub/GitLab/Bitbucket/Azure DevOps; PR review via `review.cursor.com`; **plugins/MCP marketplace** (GitHub-auth backed; 17 connector brands); **routines/automations** (cron + webhooks); **teach-by-recording** (screen recording with event timeline); **voice calls** (x.ai realtime WS); **secrets vault** (GitHub/Slack/Google/Webhook/CLI/Entra — \"values never shown to your Bot\"); **virtual card approvals via Stripe Link**; iMessage read/send (Full Disk Access); Chrome cookie import; 1Password CLI op-launcher; 23 locale catalogs (hi…bn) + 55 `.lproj`; developer palette with Statsig flag overrides and \"Capture Backend RPC Trace (2 min)\".\n\n## 7. Deliverables & how to go further\n\n- `analysis/extracted/` — full asar extraction (`dist/`, `package.json`)\n- `analysis/app.asar.unpacked/` — native Mach-O binaries (signer, op-launcher, tree-sitter, cursor-proclist)\n- `analysis/sections/sec-{main,daemon,coord,render,native}.md` — 1,102 lines of evidence-tagged findings\n- `analysis/RE-REPORT.md` — this file\n- Case artifacts: `reverse-skill-main/work/grok-bot-re/` (route-scope, case scope)\n\n**Top follow-ups (highest-value, cheapest-first):**\n1. Locate the sandbox helper binary (`getSandboxBinary` resolver) — likely downloaded at runtime; the seatbelt/landlock profiles are the core containment primitive.\n2. Run the app once with `SAND_DISABLE_TELEMETRY=1` under a proxy to capture real Connect traffic and pin the service list to live API versions (`05-protocols-ipc.md` framing loop).\n3. Extract the full 40-tool registry + JSON schemas from `local-exec-daemon/main.cjs` (protobuf oneof fields 1–57 already mapped) — this is the clean-room agent contract.\n4. Dump the 2,248-string lingui catalog (`index-C6zjACOA.js`) as the authoritative feature checklist.\n5. Verify the `checksum` header algorithm against live requests (Cursor's is known; ours matches: XOR-165 rolling, base64url + suffix).\n\n**Known gaps:** OTel collector endpoint is runtime-config only; sandbox helper provenance unconfirmed; `sec-main` custom scheme names `xc/Mi/Tc` remain minified (two of them are canvas-frame/HTML-preview per call sites).\n===\nanalysis/extracted:\ndist\npackage.json\n\nanalysis/sections:\nsec-coord.md\nsec-daemon.md\nsec-main.md\nsec-native.md\nsec-render.md\n","tool_call_id":"call_7fce00d8d0d5a9d4ff52e43055d956a9"},{"role":"assistant","content":"Strong prior work already exists — Phases 1–3 (mount, asar extraction, native triage, evidence report) are done in `analysis/`. Now the task is Phase 4: source reconstruction, using the 0.18 reconstructed repo as the structural reference. Let me inspect that reference layout and the extracted sources.","reasoning_content":"Excellent — Phase 1-3 already done (prior analysis of the same 0.44.0 DMG exists). The user now says: \"start rebuild source code\" using the reference from refrence/ (which is the 0.18 reconstructed project). So the task is Phase 4: scaffold & reconstruction, using the 0.18 reconstructed repo as a template/reference.\n\nLet me look at the reference project structure to understand what \"rebuild source code\" means here — likely a clean-room reconstruction project layout similar to grok-bot-0.18-reconstructed-main.","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_bcd058aa64c2a34f9bf786dd863bc7fa","type":"function","function":{"name":"bash","arguments":"{\"command\":\"cd \\\"/Users/panda/Projects/test-local/grok-bot/refrence/grok-bot-0.18-reconstructed-main\\\"; cat README.md | head -50; echo ===; find src -maxdepth 2 -type d 2>/dev/null | head -40; echo ===; ls source 2>/dev/null | head; echo ===; cat PROVENANCE.md | head -30\"}"}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"# Grok Bot 0.18 — reconstructed and extended\n\n![Grok Bot Router settings with Codex selected and local usage totals](docs/assets/router-settings.png)\n\nThis repository is an unofficial, source-oriented reconstruction of the\npublicly shipped Grok Bot 0.18.0 macOS app.\n\nThe project began as an attempt to understand how the desktop app was put\ntogether. It now contains readable TypeScript implementations of its Electron,\nhost, coordinator, local-execution, protocol, and renderer boundaries, plus a\ndeterministic toolchain for turning those sources back into a working macOS\napplication.\n\nIt also adds a few practical experiments:\n\n- an inference router for Cursor, Claude Code, Codex, and OpenRouter;\n- Grok Bot plugin/MCP tools across the routed providers;\n- local usage tracking for routed inference;\n- an optional local Docker sandbox in place of the remote box; and\n- a reconstructed settings surface integrated into the polished shipped UI.\n\nThis is a hacking and research project, not Anysphere's original monorepo and\nnot an official Grok Bot release. Names and module boundaries inferred from a\ncompiled application may differ from the original source.\n\n## What is in the repository?\n\nThe checked-in tree contains the reviewed reconstruction, tests, manifests,\nbuild scripts, and Git LFS preservation copies of the original macOS arm64 and\nWindows x64 installers. It deliberately does **not** commit the extracted\nupstream application, build output, local credentials, or the large forensic\nrecovery workspace.\n\nThe public Grok Bot 0.18.0 application is instead treated as a pinned build\ninput. During bootstrap, the toolchain downloads it, verifies its SHA-256\nidentity, and extracts the pieces required to assemble the reconstruction.\n\nThe resulting app is a hybrid by design:\n\n- application runtimes are compiled from the readable sources under `source/`;\n- the polished shipped renderer remains the UI baseline;\n- a narrow deterministic transform adds the reconstructed Router settings UI;\n- original and patched renderer chunk hashes are recorded and verified; and\n- the finished app uses a separate bundle identifier and an ad-hoc signature.\n\nThe upstream app installed on the machine is never overwritten.\n\n### Why retain the shipped renderer?\n\nThe distributed application did not include the original frontend source or\n===\nsrc\nsrc/app\n===\nbox-exec-daemon\nelectron-dev-controls\nelectron-main\nelectron-preload\nhost\ninternal\nlocal-exec-daemon\nmime-types.d.ts\nnode-agent-coordinator\npackages\n===\n# Provenance\n\nThe reconstruction is based on the public macOS arm64 release artifact:\n\n- Product: Grok Bot\n- Version: 0.18.0\n- Upstream bundle ID: `com.anysphere.sand`\n- Electron framework: 42.1.0\n- DMG URL: `https://downloads.cursor.com/grokbot/stable/darwin-arm64/0.18.0/Grok_Bot_0.18.0.dmg`\n- DMG SHA-256: `a253ccd8aab01e083f9812a0264354c5034d8ba7f0610bbb557e82ae77d203eb`\n- Original `app.asar` SHA-256: `6665408168466f9cacc6087e917890c17f59d2e2e9c2404a5c4a59ad79c1de58`\n\nThe repository preserves the original macOS artifact above and the matching\nWindows x64 installer through Git LFS. The Windows artifact identity is:\n\n- Installer URL: `https://downloads.cursor.com/grokbot/stable/win32-x64/0.18.0/Grok_Bot_0.18.0_Setup.exe`\n- Installer SHA-256: `464079a15ef5fa8b61ccea8fffcc78f63cfcf6df65fb0ad5e725d8b95f7e437e`\n- Preservation manifest: `research-archives/original/0.18.0/artifacts.json`\n\nThe original application was Developer ID signed and notarized by Anysphere Incorporated. Reconstructed builds are intentionally given a different bundle ID and only ad-hoc signed; they do not retain or claim the upstream signature.\n\nThe shipped renderer contained optimized production bundles, not the authored\nfrontend source or source maps. The readable `frontend/` tree is therefore a\npartial evidence-backed reconstruction, while packaged builds retain the pinned\nrenderer and apply only a narrow, hash-recorded settings transform.\n\nNo upstream source-code license is implied. Do not present reconstructed\nmaterial as original source or an official build, and complete an independent\nrights review before public redistribution.\n\n","tool_call_id":"call_bcd058aa64c2a34f9bf786dd863bc7fa"},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"use subagents for scoutings"}]},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"reasoning_content":"The user wants me to use subagents for scouting. Let me check the subagent tool guidance — need to call {action:\"list\",capabilities:true} first, then dispatch scout agents. Per safety guidance: first call list capabilities, then orchestrate. The task: scout the reference project structure and the extracted 0.44.0 analysis to plan the source rebuild.\n\nLet me load pi-subagents skill? The guidance says multi-step/parallel work: exactly one top-level subagent workflow call with async:true; children launch only inside it. And first call {action:\"list\",capabilities:true}.\n\nLet me do that first.","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_5c9ab2a8cf2bf9e082d9680b79015d20","type":"function","function":{"name":"subagent","arguments":"{\"action\":\"list\",\"capabilities\":true}"}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"Executable agents (capabilities):\nUser agents\n- gitnexus-cli (user): Description: Use when the user needs to run GitNexus CLI commands like analyze/index a repo, check status, clean the index, generate a wiki, or list indexed repos. Examples: \\\"Index this repo\\\", \\\"Reanalyze the codebase\\\", \\\"Generate a wiki\\\"; Tools: default/ambient; Model: zai/glm-5.3-flash; Thinking: high\n- gitnexus-debugging (user): Description: Use when the user is debugging a bug, tracing an error, or asking why something fails. Examples: \\\"Why is X failing?\\\", \\\"Where does this error come from?\\\", \\\"Trace this bug\\\"; Tools: default/ambient; Model: zai/glm-5.3-flash; Thinking: high\n- gitnexus-exploring (user): Description: Use when the user asks how code works, wants to understand architecture, trace execution flows, or explore unfamiliar parts of the codebase. Examples: \\\"How does X work?\\\", \\\"What calls this function?\\\", \\\"Show me the auth fl